<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-delphi10000-dasypus-novemcinctus-turing-wavelength-s0> a pk:Claim ;
    rdfs:label "Schnakenberg Turing wavelength at 4.108 µm on Holocene Dasypus novemcinctus dermis (Pass 1) (Dasypus novemcinctus (nine-banded armadillo))",
        "Schnakenberg Turing wavelength at 4.108 µm on Holocene Dasypus novemcinctus dermis (Pass 1) faithfully reproduces Dasypus novemcinctus (nine-banded armadillo) morphology" ;
    dct:conformsTo <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dasypus-novemcinctus-turing-wavelength-s0-1>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dasypus-novemcinctus-turing-wavelength-s0-2>,
        <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/math-law/delphi10000-dasypus-novemcinctus-turing-wavelength-s0-3> ;
    dct:description "The simulation script(s) models/2026-delphi10000-dasypus-novemcinctus-turing-wavelength-s0.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Dasypus novemcinctus (nine-banded armadillo) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
    dct:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-dasypus-novemcinctus-turing-wavelength-s0.py> ;
    schema:about "Dasypus novemcinctus (nine-banded armadillo)" ;
    schema:additionalProperty "does_not_claim: A parameter sweep, an equal-diffusion ablation, or a Pearson/RMSE/KS fit (those are Pass 1 gaps).",
        "does_not_claim: That nine-banded armadillo osteoderm rosettes are caused by a 4.1 µm Turing instability.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Saturated activator wavelength 4.56 µm against the linear target 4.108 µm, and σ(k_c)=0.219 > 0 while σ(0)<0.",
        "reconstructed_in_script: Schnakenberg parameters a=0.10, b=0.90, D_v/D_u=20, with D_u solved so the linear wavelength is 4.108 µm on a periodic 12-wavelength patch.",
        "reported_in_source: Chen et al. (2011) report mechanical tests and microstructure of mineralized armadillo osteoderm.",
        "reported_in_source: Turing (1952) and Schnakenberg (1979) give the two-species reaction-diffusion dispersion relation.",
        "reported_in_source: Vickaryous and Hall (2006) describe five osteoderm shields and hexagonal rosettes on the Dasypus novemcinctus carapace, developed intramembranously in the dermis.",
        "transfer_boundary: The 4.108 µm figure is this loop's subcellular detail rung, not a wavelength measured by Chen or Vickaryous. The photograph is an organism-scale carapace, not a 4.1 µm micrograph." ;
    pk:anatomicalStructure "Turing pigment stripe / ocellus wavelength in Dasypus novemcinctus (Holocene (Extant))" ;
    pk:aristotelianEfficientCause "1. Chemical Turing / RD" ;
    pk:aristotelianMegistonGenos "I. Enaima (Vertebrata)" ;
    pk:aristotelianPoionForm "Stripes, Spots & Ocelli" ;
    pk:aristotelianPosonLaw "Turing Wavenumber k_c" ;
    pk:aristotelianSubalternClass "Mammalia (Zootoka)" ;
    pk:biologicalGenus "Dasypus" ;
    pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/models/2026-delphi10000-dasypus-novemcinctus-turing-wavelength-s0.py; output: docs/media/dasypus_novemcinctus_turing_wavelength_s0_comparison.png; organism: Dasypus novemcinctus (nine-banded armadillo); environment: python 3.12.3, numpy 2.5.4, scipy 1.18.1, matplotlib 3.11.2, Pillow 13.0.0; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Vickaryous and Hall (2006) describe five osteoderm shields and hexagonal rosettes on the Dasypus novemcinctus carapace, developed intramembranously in the dermis. | Chen et al. (2011) report mechanical tests and microstructure of mineralized armadillo osteoderm. | Turing (1952) and Schnakenberg (1979) give the two-species reaction-diffusion dispersion relation.; reconstructed_in_script: Schnakenberg parameters a=0.10, b=0.90, D_v/D_u=20, with D_u solved so the linear wavelength is 4.108 µm on a periodic 12-wavelength patch. | Saturated activator wavelength 4.56 µm against the linear target 4.108 µm, and σ(k_c)=0.219 > 0 while σ(0)<0.; transfer_boundary: The 4.108 µm figure is this loop's subcellular detail rung, not a wavelength measured by Chen or Vickaryous. The photograph is an organism-scale carapace, not a 4.1 µm micrograph.; does_not_claim: That nine-banded armadillo osteoderm rosettes are caused by a 4.1 µm Turing instability. | A parameter sweep, an equal-diffusion ablation, or a Pearson/RMSE/KS fit (those are Pass 1 gaps).]; phenomenon_size: 5.5 µm (5.46e-06 m, log10=-5.263 m, tier=3_subcellular_um); species_scale_ladder (10 runs from 5.5 µm to 1.2 cm): 5.5 µm:dasypus-novemcinctus-bioelectric-polarity, 17 µm:dasypus-novemcinctus-vertex-packing, 273 µm:dasypus-novemcinctus-turing-wavelength, 546 µm:dasypus-novemcinctus-clock-wavefront, 1.2 mm:dasypus-novemcinctus-morphogen-condensation, 1.5 mm:dasypus-novemcinctus-elastic-buckling, 1.7 mm:dasypus-novemcinctus-flux-canalization, 2.4 mm:dasypus-novemcinctus-branching-tree" ;
    pk:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1002/jmor.10475>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-5193(79)90042-0>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/0022-5193(81)90334-9>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jmbbm.2010.12.013>,
        <https://doi.org/10.1098/rstb.1952.0012> ;
    pk:externalAtlasDataset <https://www.gbif.org/species/search?q=Dasypus%20novemcinctus> ;
    pk:fossilLineageGroup "Iconic Vertebrate Sensory & Dermal Specializations" ;
    pk:geologicalAgeMa 0e+00 ;
